 ## Overview
-This article derives the simplified Monte Carlo estimator for the generalized path contribution function $\hat{P}$ in volumetric path tracing. It solves the problem of expressing the radiance contribution of a sampled path in terms of the specific sampling probabilities used during generation (distance, event type, and direction). It sets itself apart from its siblings by providing the foundational algebraic simplification that reduces the raw path integral estimator to a product of phase function ratios and emission, assuming delta tracking with a single majorant. This derivation underpins the basic volumetric path tracer described in [Volumetric Path Tracing](1863-volumetric-path-tracing.md).
+This article derives the simplified Monte Carlo estimator for the generalized path contribution function $\hat{P}$ for an $n$-vertex path with $m$ real-scattering vertices, based on distance sampling, event type selection, and direction sampling [S1]. It expresses the radiance contribution of a sampled path in terms of the specific sampling probabilities used during generation [S1]. It sets itself apart from its siblings by providing the foundational algebraic simplification that reduces the raw path integral estimator to a product of phase function ratios and emission, assuming delta tracking with a single majorant [S1]. This derivation underpins the basic volumetric path tracer described in [Volumetric Path Tracing](1863-volumetric-path-tracing.md) [S1].
 
